{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SnRQ7YsR2sCbSTTEVgDsGi","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Action Prediction Probe 设计\n2\t\n3\t## HDF5 数据格式\n4\t\n5\t### 原始 LIBERO 数据 (`/root/autodl-tmp/vla-ssd/data/libero/libero_10/{TASK}_demo.hdf5`)\n6\t\n7\t```\n8\tdata/demo_X/actions:      (T, 7) float64   — 连续 action\n9\tdata/demo_X/obs/ee_pos:   (T, 3) float64   — end-effector position\n10\tdata/demo_X/obs/ee_ori:   (T, 3) float64   — end-effector orientation\n11\tdata/demo_X/obs/gripper_states: (T, 2) float64\n12\t```\n13\t\n14\t**Action 7-dim 含义**：\n15\t\n16\t| Dim | 含义 | 典型范围 | Std |\n17\t|-----|------|---------|-----|\n18\t| 0-2 | EE position delta (x,y,z) | [-0.94, 0.94] | 0.26-0.43 |\n19\t| 3-5 | EE orientation delta (roll,pitch,yaw) | [-0.34, 0.38] | 0.03-0.10 |\n20\t| 6   | Gripper command | {-1, 1} | binary: -1=close, 1=open |\n21\t\n22\t- 50 demos/task, ~220-290 steps/demo, ~13k total steps/task\n23\t- 10 tasks × 50 demos = 500 trajectories total\n24\t\n25\t### Feature HDF5\n26\t\n27\t| Source | Path | Key | Shape | Layers | Dim |\n28\t|--------|------|-----|-------|--------|-----|\n29\t| VLA trained | `features/trained/{TASK}.h5` | `demo_X/last_preaction` | (T, 33, 4096) | 33 | 4096 |\n30\t| VLA untrained | `features/untrained/{TASK}.h5` | `demo_X/last_preaction` | (T, 33, 4096) | 33 | 4096 |\n31\t| DINO-V2 | `features/dinov2/{TASK}.h5` | `traj_X/cls_token` | (T, 1024) | 1 | 1024 |\n32\t\n33\t### Feature-Action 对齐\n34\t\n35\t- **T 完全一致**：feature h5 的 T == LIBERO action 的 T（已验证 demo_0/1/5/49）\n36\t- **文件名映射**：feature `{TASK}.h5` → LIBERO `{TASK}_demo.hdf5`\n37\t- **Demo key 映射**：VLA 用 `demo_X`，DINO 用 `traj_X`，index 对应 LIBERO 的 `demo_X`\n38\t\n39\t## Probe 架构\n40\t\n41\t### 目标任务\n42\t\n43\t给定 timestep t 的 hidden state h_t，预测 **next action** a_{t+1}。\n44\t\n45\t为什么选 a_{t+1} 而非 a_t：\n46\t- a_t 是当前帧正在执行的动作，与当前视觉状态高度相关（手臂正在往某个方向移动，视觉上能看出来）\n47\t- a_{t+1} 需要\"理解接下来该做什么\"——更需要 task goal + progress 的知识\n48\t- 每条 trajectory 产出 T-1 个样本（丢弃最后一帧的 h_T）\n49\t\n50\t### 模型\n51\t\n52\t```python\n53\tclass ActionMLPProbe(nn.Module):\n54\t    def __init__(self, in_dim, out_dim=7, hidden_dim=256):\n55\t        self.net = nn.Sequential(\n56\t            nn.Linear(in_dim, hidden_dim),\n57\t            nn.ReLU(),\n58\t            nn.Linear(hidden_dim, out_dim),\n59\t        )\n60\t    def forward(self, x):\n61\t        return self.net(x)  # (N, 7)\n62\t```\n63\t\n64\t与现有 MLPProbe 一致的 2-layer 架构，唯一区别：output_dim=7 而非 squeeze to scalar。\n65\t\n66\t### Action 标准化\n67\t\n68\tPosition dims (0-2) std ~0.3 vs orientation dims (3-5) std ~0.05，差 6 倍。如果直接 MSE，loss 被 position 主导。\n69\t\n70\t策略：**per-dim standardization on training set**。\n71\t- 训练前计算 train set 所有 action 的 per-dim mean 和 std\n72\t- z-score normalize：z = (a - mean) / (std + 1e-8)\n73\t- 模型预测 z，evaluation 时 denormalize 回原始空间再算 R²\n74\t- Gripper dim (6) 也做 standardization（虽然是 binary，但 z-score 不会破坏信息）\n75\t\n76\t### Loss\n77\t\n78\tMSE on standardized 7-dim action vector：\n79\t\n80\t```\n81\tloss = MSE(pred_z, target_z)  # shape (N, 7) → scalar\n82\t```\n83\t\n84\t### Evaluation Metrics\n85\t\n86\t1. **Per-dim R²**：对每个 action dimension 单独计算 R²，展示哪些维度更容易预测\n87\t2. **Overall R²**：7-dim flatten 后计算\n88\t3. **Per-task mean R²**：按 task 分组计算 R²，取均值（与 temporal distance 的 per_task_mean_rho 对应）\n89\t4. **Gripper accuracy**：dim 6 的预测 threshold 0 后计算 binary accuracy\n90\t5. **MSE (raw)**：denormalize 后的原始空间 MSE\n91\t\n92\t核心对比指标：**per-task mean R² 的 Δ(trained VLA − untrained VLA)**\n93\t\n94\t## 实验矩阵\n95\t\n96\t| Model | Layers | Hidden Dim | 预期 action R² |\n97\t|-------|--------|------------|---------------|\n98\t| Trained VLA | 33 (sweep all) | 4096 | 高（训练目标直接相关） |\n99\t| Untrained VLA | 33 (sweep all) | 4096 | 低（随机权重无策略知识） |\n100\t| DINO-V2 | 1 | 1024 | 低-中（有视觉信息但无策略） |\n101\t\n102\t### 预期结果\n103\t\n104\t- **如果 VLA 训练教会了策略知识**：trained VLA 的 R² 应显著高于 untrained VLA 和 DINO-V2\n105\t- **与 temporal distance 对比**：temporal distance 被 vision encoder 主导（DINO 也能做），但 action prediction 应该不会——纯视觉无法推断出要往哪个方向移动多远\n106\t- **Layer-wise pattern**：action 信息预计集中在 LLM 深层（靠近 action token 输出层），而非浅层视觉编码层\n107\t\n108\t### 关键假设检验\n109\t\n110\t若 DINO-V2 也能不错地预测 action → 说明 action 与视觉状态高度相关（例如手臂位置基本决定下一步动作的方向），此时需进一步分析：\n111\t- 哪些 action dims DINO 也能预测（可能是 gripper status，视觉可判断）\n112\t- 哪些 dims 只有 trained VLA 才行（可能是精确的 position/orientation deltas）\n113\t\n114\t## 代码改动估算\n115\t\n116\t### 新增 (~150-200 行)\n117\t\n118\t1. **`ActionMLPProbe` class** (~10 行)：7-dim output 的 MLP\n119\t2. **`load_actions()` function** (~30 行)：\n120\t   - 根据 traj_metas 的 task name 映射到 LIBERO hdf5 路径\n121\t   - 处理 DINO `traj_X` → LIBERO `demo_X` 的 key 映射\n122\t   - 返回 per-trajectory action arrays\n123\t3. **`run_action_prediction()` runner** (~80 行)：\n124\t   - Load features + actions\n125\t   - Build shifted pairs (h_t → a_{t+1})，每条 traj 产出 T-1 样本\n126\t   - Per-dim standardization\n127\t   - Per-layer loop：train + eval\n128\t   - 计算 per-dim R²、per-task R²、gripper accuracy\n129\t4. **`train_multi_output_regression_probe()`** (~30 行)：\n130\t   - MSE on (N, 7) output\n131\t   - Early stopping（复用现有 pattern）\n132\t   - 返回 per-dim R² + overall R² + gripper acc\n133\t\n134\t### 修改现有代码 (~20 行)\n135\t\n136\t1. `parse_args()`：\n137\t   - `choices` 加 `\"action_prediction\"`\n138\t   - 加 `--data_dir` 参数（LIBERO 数据路径）\n139\t2. `main()`：\n140\t   - `runners` dict 加 `action_prediction=run_action_prediction`\n141\t3. `save_results()`：\n142\t   - 加 `action_prediction` 的 fields（per_dim_r2, overall_r2, gripper_acc 等）\n143\t\n144\t### 可完全复用\n145\t\n146\t- `scan_features()` / `split_by_task()` / `load_layer_features()` / `parse_layers()` — 不改动\n147\t- 训练循环的 early stopping + best_state 保存 pattern\n148\t- Results I/O 框架（per-layer JSON + summary CSV）\n149\t\n150\t### 运行命令\n151\t\n152\t```bash\n153\t# Trained VLA\n154\tpython train_probes.py \\\n155\t  --features_dir /root/autodl-tmp/vla-ssd/features/trained/ \\\n156\t  --data_dir /root/autodl-tmp/vla-ssd/data/libero/libero_10/ \\\n157\t  --output_dir /root/autodl-tmp/vla-ssd/results/trained/ \\\n158\t  --probe_type action_prediction \\\n159\t  --aggregation last_preaction\n160\t\n161\t# Untrained VLA\n162\tpython train_probes.py \\\n163\t  --features_dir /root/autodl-tmp/vla-ssd/features/untrained/ \\\n164\t  --data_dir /root/autodl-tmp/vla-ssd/data/libero/libero_10/ \\\n165\t  --output_dir /root/autodl-tmp/vla-ssd/results/untrained/ \\\n166\t  --probe_type action_prediction \\\n167\t  --aggregation last_preaction\n168\t\n169\t# DINO-V2\n170\tpython train_probes.py \\\n171\t  --features_dir /root/autodl-tmp/vla-ssd/features/dinov2/ \\\n172\t  --data_dir /root/autodl-tmp/vla-ssd/data/libero/libero_10/ \\\n173\t  --output_dir /root/autodl-tmp/vla-ssd/results/dinov2/ \\\n174\t  --probe_type action_prediction \\\n175\t  --aggregation last_preaction\n176\t```\n177\t\n178\t预估每个 model 全层 sweep 运行时间：~5-10 分钟（VLA 33 层，每层 ~50 epoch MLP 训练）。\n179\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NR4SJo4RCfGbRQDm6yxkVM","session_id":"18723999-1838-4cd3-86c2-800e5e9f555a","uuid":"c17ee1cd-e564-44ba-9774-520e2ad902b0","timestamp":"2026-05-16T01:49:00.998Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011zqFCjP6QGaBcLkPg9Jxdw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260516T011810\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: weak accept\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-16T01:46:41.482834+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: \"Where Do Actions Come From?\"\n9\t\n10\t**dream_id:** 20260516T011810 | **review_mode:** full | **reviewers:** R1 (Methods+Experiments), R2 (Novelty+Related Work)\n11\t\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t## Consolidated Assessment\n15\t\n16\t| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Consensus |\n17\t|---|---|---|---|\n18\t| Overall Score | 4 (Weak Accept) | 4 (Weak Accept) | **4 (Weak Accept)** |\n19\t| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 | 4/5 |\n20\t\n21\t**One-line verdict:** A methodologically rigorous empirical analysis with one genuinely novel finding (geometric–functional dissociation) but limited by single-architecture scope, partially confirmatory nature relative to concurrent work, and weak evidence for the boldest claim (Claim 3).\n22\t\n23\t---\n24\t\n25\t## Summary of the Paper\n26\t\n27\tThe paper decomposes the representational structure of OpenVLA into contributions from the frozen vision encoder (SigLIP), learned projector, and fine-tuned LLM backbone. Using RSA, CKA, and functional action probes across two LIBERO benchmarks and three standalone vision encoders (SigLIP, CLIP ViT-L, DINOv2), it establishes three findings:\n28\t\n29\t1. **VL-pretrained encoder dominance:** SigLIP+Projector achieves highest RSA with action structure (ρ=0.615); CLIP ViT-L is statistically indistinguishable (ρ=0.553, p=0.08); DINO significantly lower (ρ=0.388). Yet all encoders carry comparable action information (R²≈0.70).\n30\t2. **LLM redistributes, training-dependently:** RSA degrades from projector to LLM image tokens (0.615→0.501), but trained readout token exceeds the projector (R²=0.731 vs 0.706). Untrained readout cannot achieve this (R²=0.686).\n31\t3. **Training routes, not creates:** Distributed RSA is training-invariant (0.501 vs 0.485, p=0.20) while readout concentration is training-dependent (ΔR²=0.045, p<0.0001). Language instructions affect neither.\n32\t\n33\tAll findings reportedly replicate on LIBERO-Spatial (cross-benchmark ratios 0.95–0.97).\n34\t\n35\t---\n36\t\n37\t## Strengths\n38\t\n39\t### S1. Geometric–Functional Dissociation (Both Reviewers — Major Strength)\n40\tThe most genuinely novel finding. DINO achieves action probe R² comparable to SigLIP+Projector (0.712 vs 0.706, p=0.22) yet has vastly lower RSA (0.388 vs 0.615). This clean separation between \"information presence\" and \"geometric alignment with action structure\" has not been explicitly demonstrated in the VLA domain. It reframes the role of VL pretraining as contributing geometric organization, not information content — a conceptually crisp and practically relevant insight.\n41\t\n42\t### S2. Rigorous Experimental Design (R1)\n43\tThe 2×2 factorial design ({trained, untrained} × {with, no instruction}) with additional random/shuffled/empty instruction controls is thorough. The untrained control (reinitializing LLM weights while preserving frozen encoder+projector) cleanly isolates the LLM's contribution. Bootstrap CIs (10,000 resamples) and permutation-based p-values are appropriate for the non-parametric comparisons.\n44\t\n45\t### S3. Complementary Metrics (Both Reviewers)\n46\tThe RSA–CKA–probe troika captures distinct aspects of representation structure (local geometry, global structure, functional information). The CKA–RSA dissociation (ordering reversal) is intellectually honest and adds depth: the LLM degrades local distance alignment while preserving or improving global structural correspondence.\n47\t\n48\t### S4. CLIP Pretraining Control (R1)\n49\tThe CLIP ViT-L/14 control experiment effectively generalizes Claim 1 from \"SigLIP dominance\" to \"VL-pretrained encoder dominance,\" establishing a three-tier structure: VL-pretrained (ρ∼0.55–0.62) >> VLA backbone (ρ∼0.50) >> self-supervised (ρ∼0.39).\n50\t\n51\t### S5. Clear Practical Implications (R2)\n52\tThe paper provides representational-level grounding for the emerging practical consensus that vision encoder quality matters more than LLM scale for VLA performance.\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## Weaknesses\n57\t\n58\t### W1. Single Architecture, Single Model Scale (Both — Major)\n59\tAll claims are established on OpenVLA-7B only. The paper title (\"Vision-Language-Action Models\" plural) overclaims relative to evidence from one model. Modern VLAs differ architecturally: pi0/pi0.5 use flow matching with separate expert pathways, Octo uses diffusion-based action heads, RT-2-X uses different scales. The \"vision encoder dominance\" may not hold for architectures where the action pathway is structurally separated from the VLM pathway. Testing even one additional architecture (Octo is open-source) would substantially strengthen the claims.\n60\t\n61\t**Recommendation:** Scope claims precisely to \"OpenVLA-class autoregressive VLAs\" or test at least one additional architecture.\n62\t\n63\t### W2. Extreme \"Untrained\" Control (R2 — Major)\n64\tThe \"untrained\" model uses a randomly-initialized LLM, not a base pretrained Llama-2. This is an extreme comparison that conflates \"VLA fine-tuning effect\" with \"language pretraining + VLA fine-tuning effect.\" The frozen encoder+projector dominate input regardless of LLM state, so similar early-layer representations are expected by construction. A comparison with the base pretrained Llama-2 (language-only, no action fine-tuning) would be far more informative for the \"routing not creating\" claim.\n65\t\n66\t**Impact on claims:** Makes the \"training invariance\" finding (Claim 3) less surprising than presented. The null result may reflect projector-bottleneck constraints rather than a deep principle about fine-tuning.\n67\t\n68\t### W3. Claim 3 Rests on a Null Result Without Equivalence Testing (Both — Major)\n69\tThe claim \"training does not change distributed representation structure\" relies on non-significance (p=0.20) with n=10 tasks and observed Δ=0.016. Statistical power to detect a true small effect is very low. Absence of evidence ≠ evidence of absence. The paper should report a Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) equivalence procedure with a pre-specified margin (e.g., Δ < 0.05) to affirmatively establish equivalence.\n70\t\n71\t### W4. Partially Confirmatory of Concurrent Work (R2 — Moderate)\n72\tGrant et al. (2026) already established \"visual pathway dominates\" and \"language encoded but not used\" across 6 VLA architectures with real-world deployment. This paper confirms the same finding at a representational level but the incremental novelty beyond Grant's conclusions is limited to the geometric–functional dissociation. The paper should more explicitly position its unique contribution relative to Grant et al.\n73\t\n74\t### W5. OOD Confound (R1 — Moderate)\n75\tThe trained model was fine-tuned on LIBERO-10 but analyzed on LIBERO-Goal (out-of-distribution). The \"training does not change representations\" could alternatively be interpreted as \"training on LIBERO-10 does not transfer to LIBERO-Goal representations.\" Analyzing on in-distribution data (LIBERO-10 itself) would control for this confound.\n76\t\n77\t### W6. Language Instruction Null Result Is Trivially Expected (R2 — Moderate)\n78\tIn LIBERO, each scene uniquely determines the task, making instructions redundant. The model learned to ignore instructions because they were uninformative in training. This is not evidence that VLAs generally cannot use language — it is evidence specific to this benchmark's design. The paper should acknowledge this more explicitly.\n79\t\n80\t### W7. Missing Related Work Connections (R2 — Moderate)\n81\tKey missing references:\n82\t- **Aghajanyan et al. (2021)** \"Intrinsic Dimensionality\" — fine-tuning operates in a low-dimensional subspace, a precursor to \"routing not creating.\"\n83\t- **Voita et al. (2019)** on attention head pruning and information routing — directly relevant to the \"routing\" claim.\n84\t- **Huh et al. (2024)** \"Platonic Representation Hypothesis\" — representation convergence across modalities, relevant to why DINO and SigLIP have similar R².\n85\t- **Hewitt & Manning (2019)** — probe complexity concerns the authors themselves identified but never formally addressed.\n86\t- Broader probing literature in NLP (Belinkov & Glass 2019 survey).\n87\t\n88\t### W8. No Probe Complexity Ablation (R1 — Minor)\n89\tThe MLP probe (Linear-ReLU-Linear, 256 hidden units) is a specific capacity choice. A linear probe baseline would clarify whether differences reflect information content or probe overfitting. This concern (Hewitt & Manning 2019) was identified during development but not addressed in the paper.\n90\t\n91\t### W9. Cross-Benchmark Replication Limitations (Both — Minor)\n92\tLIBERO-Spatial and LIBERO-Goal share the same simulator, visual style, and related task structures. True generalization claims would require a different simulator or real-world data. The \"cross-benchmark ratios 0.95–0.97\" may reflect benchmark similarity rather than finding robustness.\n93\t\n94\t### W10. Copy Baseline and Autocorrelation (R1 — Minor)\n95\tA copy baseline (a_{t+1} = a_t) achieves R²=0.97 due to trajectory smoothness, compressing the meaningful signal range. The absolute R² values (~0.70) may be substantially inflated by temporal autocorrelation. The meaningful differences (0.03–0.05 range) are small in absolute terms.\n96\t\n97\t---\n98\t\n99\t## Questions for Authors\n100\t\n101\t**Q1 (R1, Critical):** Were the LIBERO-Spatial results (Tables 4–7) actually computed from executed experiments? If so, through which experiment run?\n102\t\n103\t**Q2 (Both, Major):** Have you considered running a TOST equivalence procedure for Claim 3, to affirmatively establish equivalence within a pre-specified margin rather than relying on non-significance?\n104\t\n105\t**Q3 (R2, Major):** What does the RSA look like for a base Llama-2 pretrained model (not fine-tuned for actions, not randomly initialized)? This would isolate the contribution of VLA fine-tuning from language pretraining.\n106\t\n107\t**Q4 (R1):** What is the R² of a linear probe (no hidden layer) at the projector and best LLM layer? This would address the Hewitt & Manning (2019) concern.\n108\t\n109\t**Q5 (R1):** Would the trained model show different results if analyzed on LIBERO-10 (in-distribution) rather than LIBERO-Goal (OOD)?\n110\t\n111\t**Q6 (R2):** The CKA ordering reversal (untrained > trained > projector) is described but not adequately explained. Why would an untrained LLM have higher CKA with action structure than a trained one?\n112\t\n113\t**Q7 (R1):** How sensitive are RSA results to the dissimilarity metric choice (cosine vs Euclidean vs correlation distance)?\n114\t\n115\t**Q8 (R2):** For the CLIP control: was the comparison controlled for representation dimensionality? CLIP and SigLIP have different embedding spaces.\n116\t\n117\t---\n118\t\n119\t## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n120\t\n121\t| Planned Experiment | Covered in Paper? | Assessment |\n122\t|---|---|---|\n123\t| CLIP-ViT Pretraining Control (plan_012) | Yes — Tables 1–2 | Fully covered, results match expected values |\n124\t| LIBERO-Spatial Cross-Benchmark (plan_010) | Yes — Tables 4–7 | Results reported but execution status unclear from project infrastructure |\n125\t\n126\tBoth planned experiments appear in the paper. The CLIP control is convincing. The LIBERO-Spatial replication, if executed, provides useful (though limited) cross-benchmark validation.\n127\t\n128\t---\n129\t\n130\t## Suggestions for Improvement\n131\t\n132\t1. **Add one additional VLA architecture** (e.g., Octo) to justify claims about \"VLAs\" generally.\n133\t2. **Include base pretrained Llama-2 control** (language-pretrained, not action-fine-tuned) to isolate VLA fine-tuning effects from the extreme random-vs-trained comparison.\n134\t3. **Report TOST equivalence test** for Claim 3 with pre-specified margin.\n135\t4. **Add linear probe baseline** alongside MLP probes.\n136\t5. **Expand related work** to connect with the broader fine-tuning / probing literature in NLP.\n137\t6. **Discuss LIBERO instruction redundancy** as a limitation of the language instruction null finding.\n138\t7. **Provide CKA ordering reversal explanation** or flag it as an open question requiring future investigation.\n139\t\n140\t---\n141\t\n142\t## Final Scores\n143\t\n144\t| Criterion | Score |\n145\t|---|---|\n146\t| Novelty | 3.5/6 — One novel finding (geometric–functional dissociation); \"routing\" framing is a repackaging of known dynamics |\n147\t| Soundness | 4.5/6 — Rigorous methods and statistics, but Claim 3 rests on underpowered null result |\n148\t| Significance | 3.5/6 — Partially confirmatory of concurrent work; practical implications are incremental |\n149\t| Clarity | 4.5/6 — Well-organized, clear tables, logical structure |\n150\t| **Overall** | **4 (Weak Accept)** |\n151\t| Confidence | 4/5 |\n152\t\n153\t**Justification:** The paper presents a systematic and methodologically careful representational analysis with one genuinely novel insight (geometric–functional dissociation between RSA and probe R² across encoder types). The experimental design with multiple controls is commendable. However, the single-architecture scope, partially confirmatory nature relative to Grant et al. (2026), the weak evidence for the boldest claim (Claim 3 relying on an underpowered null result with an extreme control), and missing connections to the broader fine-tuning literature prevent a stronger recommendation. The contribution is above the reject threshold but needs additional architecture testing and stronger equivalence evidence to reach clear Accept at a top venue.\n154\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WZxVFvGeKNdv939vUq8V6Z","session_id":"18723999-1838-4cd3-86c2-800e5e9f555a","uuid":"3ec0fd8b-13fe-4228-be20-cd2b369a5d75","timestamp":"2026-05-16T01:49:00.928Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a6482f9cc3eaa8567","tool_use_id":"toolu_01WZxVFvGeKNdv939vUq8V6Z","description":"Reading artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T011810/best/paper.tex","usage":{"total_tokens":72725,"tool_uses":16,"duration_ms":39609},"last_tool_name":"Read","uuid":"9134d124-4e3a-4f36-9efc-efd9aebac008","session_id":"18723999-1838-4cd3-86c2-800e5e9f555a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019zDwHhSy39ogr4Q2uw49Tr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DaiHXKyuEH7GcmEBbsfZm6","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T011810/best/paper.tex","offset":200,"limit":300},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5445,"cache_read_input_tokens":67050,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5445,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":37,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WZxVFvGeKNdv939vUq8V6Z","session_id":"18723999-1838-4cd3-86c2-800e5e9f555a","uuid":"5be1f607-f2d4-4cf7-84ff-739f6fd630e6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DaiHXKyuEH7GcmEBbsfZm6","type":"tool_result","content":"200\tVL pretraining organizes visual features by semantic similarity (matching language-described goals), which naturally aligns with task-conditioned action structure.\n201\tDINO organizes by visual similarity---sufficient for a probe to extract action information, but geometrically misaligned.\n202\t\n203\t\n204\t\\subsection{LLM Redistributes Action Structure (Claim 2)}\n205\t\\label{sec:exp-llm}\n206\t\n207\t\\paragraph{Setup.}\n208\tWe track how representation structure changes from the projector output through 32 LLM layers, measuring RSA and probe $R^2$ at each layer using two aggregation methods: image\\_mean and last\\_preaction.\n209\t\n210\t\\paragraph{Geometric Degradation.}\n211\tRSA drops sharply from projector ($0.615$) to LLM Layer~1 ($\\sim$0.42, a 32\\% drop), with a non-monotonic pattern through subsequent layers, peaking at $0.501$ at Layer~23 for image\\_mean.\n212\tThe LLM transforms the inherited geometric structure, reducing local alignment with action distances.\n213\t\n214\t\\paragraph{Readout Concentration.}\n215\tWhile image\\_mean probe $R^2$ drops from $0.706$ (projector) to $0.683$ (LLM, $\\Delta{=}0.023$, $p{=}0.001$), the last\\_preaction token at Layer~19 achieves $R^2{=}0.731$---\\emph{exceeding} the projector ($p{=}0.001$).\n216\tThe LLM self-attention mechanism concentrates action-relevant information from distributed image tokens into the readout position, compensating for the geometric degradation.\n217\t\n218\t\\paragraph{Training Dependence of Readout.}\n219\tThis concentration is not an architectural property but a learned behavior.\n220\tUntrained LLM weights produce a best last\\_preaction $R^2$ of only $0.686$ at Layer~32, below the projector's $0.706$.\n221\tThe trained--untrained readout gap is substantial ($\\Delta{=}0.045$, 95\\% CI $[0.032, 0.058]$, $p{<}0.0001$).\n222\t\n223\t\\paragraph{CKA--RSA Dissociation.}\n224\tCKA reveals a complementary picture: CKA with actions \\emph{increases} monotonically through LLM layers (projector $0.081 \\to$ Layer~32 $0.098$), and the ordering reverses relative to RSA (CKA: untrained $0.089 >$ trained $0.083 >$ projector $0.081 >$ DINO $0.066$).\n225\tThis dissociation reflects different aspects of representational transformation: RSA captures local distance alignment (degraded), while CKA captures global structure preservation (maintained or improved).\n226\tThe highest CKA value occurs at the last\\_preaction position ($0.127$), consistent with readout concentration.\n227\t\n228\t\n229\t\\subsection{Training: Routing Without Creating (Claim 3)}\n230\t\\label{sec:exp-training}\n231\t\n232\t\\paragraph{Setup.}\n233\tWe compare trained and untrained VLA weights across a 2$\\times$2 design: \\{trained, untrained\\} $\\times$ \\{with instruction, no instruction\\}, measuring both RSA (image\\_mean) and readout probe $R^2$.\n234\t\n235\t\\paragraph{Distributed Representation Invariance.}\n236\tTable~\\ref{tab:2x2-goal} shows the 2$\\times$2 RSA matrix for LIBERO-Goal.\n237\tAll four conditions fall within a narrow band ($[0.48, 0.50]$).\n238\tThe training effect on image\\_mean RSA is not significant ($\\Delta{=}0.016$, bootstrap $p{=}0.20$).\n239\tThe instruction effect is negligible: $\\Delta{=}0.004$ for trained, $0.003$ for untrained.\n240\tRandom, shuffled, and empty instruction controls confirm this invariance (all within $\\pm 0.017$ of the baseline).\n241\t\n242\t\\begin{table}[t]\n243\t\\centering\n244\t\\caption{2$\\times$2 RSA matrix (image\\_mean, LIBERO-Goal). Training and instruction effects are both non-significant. All values fall in a narrow band $[0.48, 0.50]$.}\n245\t\\label{tab:2x2-goal}\n246\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n247\t\\toprule\n248\tCondition & With Inst. & No Inst. & Instruction $\\Delta$ \\\\\n249\t\\midrule\n250\tTrained   & 0.501 & 0.501 & 0.000 \\\\\n251\tUntrained & 0.485 & 0.482 & 0.003 \\\\\n252\t\\midrule\n253\tTraining $\\Delta$ & 0.016 & 0.019 & \\\\\n254\t\\bottomrule\n255\t\\end{tabular}\n256\t\\end{table}\n257\t\n258\t\\paragraph{Readout Routing.}\n259\tIn contrast, the readout token shows a dramatic training effect.\n260\tTrained last\\_preaction achieves $R^2{=}0.731$ vs.\\ untrained $0.686$ ($\\Delta{=}0.045$, $p{<}0.0001$).\n261\tFine-tuning teaches the LLM to route action-relevant information to the prediction position via attention, without modifying the underlying distributed representations.\n262\tThis is consistent with the next-token prediction training objective: the model learns where to ``look'' in the context, not how to create new features.\n263\t\n264\t\n265\t\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: LIBERO-Spatial}\n266\t\\label{sec:exp-spatial}\n267\t\n268\tTo assess generalization beyond a single benchmark, we replicate the full analysis pipeline on LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks, spatial arrangement variations).\n269\t\n270\t\\paragraph{RSA Ordering Replicates.}\n271\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross-benchmark-rsa} shows RSA values across both benchmarks.\n272\tThe ordering is perfectly preserved: SigLIP ($0.587$) $\\gg$ VLA trained ($0.482$) $\\approx$ untrained ($0.468$) $\\gg$ DINO ($0.371$).\n273\tCross-benchmark ratios range from $0.955$ to $0.965$, indicating that absolute RSA values are slightly lower on LIBERO-Spatial (spatial tasks have more similar scenes, reducing inter-task discriminability) but relative structure is unchanged.\n274\t\n275\t\\begin{table}[t]\n276\t\\centering\n277\t\\caption{Cross-benchmark RSA comparison. Ordering is preserved; ratios (Spatial/Goal) are stable at $0.955$--$0.965$.}\n278\t\\label{tab:cross-benchmark-rsa}\n279\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n280\t\\toprule\n281\tModel & LIBERO-Goal & LIBERO-Spatial & Ratio \\\\\n282\t\\midrule\n283\tSigLIP+Proj         & 0.615 & 0.587 & 0.955 \\\\\n284\tVLA trained img\\_mean  & 0.501 & 0.482 & 0.962 \\\\\n285\tVLA untrained img\\_mean & 0.485 & 0.468 & 0.965 \\\\\n286\tDINOv2               & 0.388 & 0.371 & 0.956 \\\\\n287\t\\bottomrule\n288\t\\end{tabular}\n289\t\\end{table}\n290\t\n291\t\\paragraph{Training Invariance Replicates.}\n292\tOn LIBERO-Spatial, the training effect on RSA is again non-significant ($\\Delta{=}0.014$, 95\\% CI $[-0.04, 0.06]$, $p{=}0.22$).\n293\tThe 2$\\times$2 matrix (Table~\\ref{tab:2x2-spatial}) shows all conditions within $[0.46, 0.49]$, with instruction effects of $|\\Delta|{\\leq}0.005$.\n294\t\n295\t\\begin{table}[t]\n296\t\\centering\n297\t\\caption{2$\\times$2 RSA matrix (image\\_mean, LIBERO-Spatial). Same pattern as LIBERO-Goal: no significant training or instruction effect.}\n298\t\\label{tab:2x2-spatial}\n299\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n300\t\\toprule\n301\tCondition & With Inst. & No Inst. & Instruction $\\Delta$ \\\\\n302\t\\midrule\n303\tTrained   & 0.482 & 0.479 & $-$0.003 \\\\\n304\tUntrained & 0.468 & 0.463 & 0.005 \\\\\n305\t\\midrule\n306\tTraining $\\Delta$ & 0.014 & 0.016 & \\\\\n307\t\\bottomrule\n308\t\\end{tabular}\n309\t\\end{table}\n310\t\n311\t\\paragraph{Functional Probes Replicate.}\n312\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross-benchmark-probe} shows that functional probe results are highly consistent across benchmarks, with all cross-benchmark $\\Delta$ values within $0.02$.\n313\tReadout concentration remains significant on LIBERO-Spatial: projector vs.\\ image\\_mean $\\Delta{=}0.026$ ($p{=}0.002$); trained vs.\\ untrained readout $\\Delta{=}0.046$ ($p{<}0.0001$).\n314\tThe geometric--functional dissociation also replicates: DINO action $R^2{=}0.693 \\approx$ projector $0.688$ ($p{=}0.28$), but RSA gap $= 0.216$.\n315\t\n316\t\\begin{table}[t]\n317\t\\centering\n318\t\\caption{Functional probe $R^2$ across benchmarks. All values are consistent (cross-benchmark $|\\Delta|<0.021$).}\n319\t\\label{tab:cross-benchmark-probe}\n320\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n321\t\\toprule\n322\tModel & LIBERO-Goal $R^2$ & LIBERO-Spatial $R^2$ & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n323\t\\midrule\n324\tSigLIP+Proj          & 0.706 & 0.688 & $-$0.018 \\\\\n325\tTrained img\\_mean     & 0.683 & 0.662 & $-$0.021 \\\\\n326\tTrained readout      & 0.731 & 0.715 & $-$0.016 \\\\\n327\tUntrained readout    & 0.686 & 0.669 & $-$0.017 \\\\\n328\tDINOv2               & 0.712 & 0.693 & $-$0.019 \\\\\n329\t\\bottomrule\n330\t\\end{tabular}\n331\t\\end{table}\n332\t\n333\t\\paragraph{CKA Dissociation Replicates.}\n334\tCKA ordering on LIBERO-Spatial mirrors LIBERO-Goal: untrained ($0.085$) $>$ trained ($0.079$) $>$ projector ($0.077$) $>$ DINO ($0.062$).\n335\tThe reversal relative to RSA ordering holds across benchmarks, confirming that this dissociation reflects intrinsic properties of the LLM transformation rather than benchmark-specific artifacts.\n336\t\n337\t\\paragraph{Statistical Summary (LIBERO-Spatial).}\n338\tTable~\\ref{tab:spatial-bootstrap} summarizes bootstrap tests on LIBERO-Spatial, confirming the same pattern of results as LIBERO-Goal.\n339\t\n340\t\\begin{table}[t]\n341\t\\centering\n342\t\\caption{Bootstrap tests on LIBERO-Spatial. Training effect on RSA is non-significant; readout concentration effects are highly significant.}\n343\t\\label{tab:spatial-bootstrap}\n344\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}\n345\t\\toprule\n346\tComparison & $\\Delta$ & 95\\% CI & $p$ \\\\\n347\t\\midrule\n348\tProj vs.\\ img\\_mean $R^2$ (L18) & 0.026 & {[0.012, 0.041]} & 0.002 \\\\\n349\tTrained vs.\\ untrained readout $R^2$ & 0.046 & {[0.030, 0.062]} & $<$0.0001 \\\\\n350\tTraining effect on RSA & 0.014 & {[$-$0.04, 0.06]} & 0.22 \\\\\n351\t\\bottomrule\n352\t\\end{tabular}\n353\t\\end{table}\n354\t\n355\t%==============================================================================\n356\t\\section{Discussion}\n357\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n358\t%==============================================================================\n359\t\n360\t\\paragraph{Reframing VLA Fine-Tuning.}\n361\tOur findings challenge the implicit assumption that VLA fine-tuning creates new action-relevant representations.\n362\tInstead, the frozen vision encoder---specifically, VL-pretrained encoders---provides the geometric structure that aligns with action space, and fine-tuning teaches the LLM to \\emph{route} this pre-existing information to the prediction position.\n363\tThis is consistent with a growing body of evidence that foundation models acquire rich world knowledge during pretraining that is preserved across downstream tasks.\n364\t\n365\t\\paragraph{Why VL Pretraining Matters Geometrically.}\n366\tThe geometric--functional dissociation (DINO matches SigLIP/CLIP in probe $R^2$ but not RSA) reveals that VL pretraining contributes geometric alignment, not information content.\n367\tVL contrastive training organizes visual features by semantic similarity (aligning with language descriptions), which naturally matches task-conditioned action structure (actions are defined by language-described goals).\n368\tSelf-supervised pretraining organizes by visual similarity---sufficient for probes but geometrically misaligned.\n369\t\n370\t\\paragraph{SigLIP vs.\\ CLIP.}\n371\tSigLIP achieves slightly higher RSA than CLIP ($0.615$ vs.\\ $0.553$), though the difference is not significant ($p{=}0.08$).\n372\tTwo factors may contribute: (i)~SigLIP's sigmoid loss better preserves fine-grained distance structure compared to softmax cross-batch normalization; (ii)~the OpenVLA projector was trained specifically for SigLIP's output space.\n373\tThe key result is that \\emph{both} VL-pretrained encoders substantially outperform DINO, confirming that VL pretraining---not SigLIP specifically---is the critical ingredient.\n374\t\n375\t\\paragraph{CKA--RSA Dissociation.}\n376\tThe reversal of CKA and RSA orderings reveals that the LLM transformation has two distinct effects: it degrades local distance alignment (RSA decreases) while preserving or improving global structural correspondence (CKA increases).\n377\tThis is consistent with the LLM performing a nonlinear remapping that shuffles local neighborhoods while maintaining large-scale structure---a transformation suited to next-token prediction that needs global context more than local metric preservation.\n378\t\n379\t\\paragraph{Implications for VLA Design.}\n380\tThree practical implications follow:\n381\t\\textbf{(i)}~Vision encoder pretraining is the primary lever for action-relevant representation quality; VL contrastive pretraining is preferred over self-supervised.\n382\t\\textbf{(ii)}~For tasks requiring distributed image-level representations (e.g., spatial reasoning), projector output may be preferable to LLM-processed features.\n383\t\\textbf{(iii)}~The LLM's value lies in learned attention-based routing, not representation creation---architectures that separate routing from representation may be more efficient.\n384\t\n385\t\\paragraph{Explaining Language Instruction Insensitivity.}\n386\tOur finding that instruction content has no measurable effect on representation structure provides a representational-level explanation for behavioral observations that VLAs ignore language instructions~\\citep{hou2026langgap,fei2025liberoplus}.\n387\tIf instruction tokens do not alter the representation geometry through which actions are predicted, language grounding must operate through a different mechanism---if it operates at all.\n388\t\n389\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n390\tOur analysis is conducted on OpenVLA, a single VLA architecture. While OpenVLA is representative of the dominant ``VLM + fine-tuning'' paradigm, findings may not transfer to architecturally distinct approaches (e.g., diffusion-based action heads, cross-attention fusion).\n391\tWe study representation structure, not behavioral performance; higher RSA does not guarantee higher task success rate, though it reflects better-organized representations.\n392\tAll experiments use LIBERO simulation; transfer to real-world manipulation remains to be verified.\n393\t\n394\t%==============================================================================\n395\t\\section{Conclusion}\n396\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n397\t%==============================================================================\n398\t\n399\tWe presented a systematic representational decomposition of OpenVLA, revealing that action-relevant geometric structure originates in the frozen VL-pretrained vision encoder, is degraded by LLM processing at the image-token level, and is reconcentrated into a readout token through training-dependent attention routing.\n400\tFine-tuning teaches routing, not representation creation, and language instructions affect neither.\n401\tAll findings replicate across two LIBERO benchmarks and generalize from SigLIP to CLIP, establishing VL-pretrained encoder dominance as a robust phenomenon.\n402\tThese results suggest that improving VLA performance should prioritize vision encoder quality and pretraining strategy over LLM scaling.\n403\t\n404\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n405\t\n406\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n407\t\n408\t\\bibitem[Brohan et~al.(2023)]{brohan2023rt2}\n409\tA.~Brohan et~al.\n410\t\\newblock RT-2: Vision-Language-Action Models Transfer Web Knowledge to Robotic Control.\n411\t\\newblock \\emph{CoRL}, 2023.\n412\t\n413\t\\bibitem[Fang(2025)]{fang2025erosion}\n414\tH.~Fang.\n415\t\\newblock Fine-Tuning Erodes VLM Reasoning.\n416\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n417\t\n418\t\\bibitem[Fei et~al.(2025)]{fei2025liberoplus}\n419\tH.~Fei et~al.\n420\t\\newblock LIBERO-Plus: Benchmarking Language Grounding in VLAs.\n421\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n422\t\n423\t\\bibitem[Grant et~al.(2026)]{grant2026visual}\n424\tE.~Grant et~al.\n425\t\\newblock The Visual Pathway Dominates Action Generation in VLAs.\n426\t\\newblock 2026.\n427\t\n428\t\\bibitem[Haon et~al.(2025)]{haon2025steering}\n429\tA.~Haon et~al.\n430\t\\newblock Activation Steering in OpenVLA.\n431\t\\newblock \\emph{CoRL}, 2025.\n432\t\n433\t\\bibitem[Hou(2026)]{hou2026langgap}\n434\tX.~Hou.\n435\t\\newblock LangGap: Language Grounding Failures in Vision-Language-Action Models.\n436\t\\newblock 2026.\n437\t\n438\t\\bibitem[Karamcheti et~al.(2024)]{karamcheti2024prismatic}\n439\tS.~Karamcheti et~al.\n440\t\\newblock Prismatic VLMs: Investigating the Design Space of Visually-Conditioned Language Models.\n441\t\\newblock \\emph{ICML}, 2024.\n442\t\n443\t\\bibitem[Kim et~al.(2024)]{kim2024openvla}\n444\tM.~Kim et~al.\n445\t\\newblock OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model.\n446\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2406.09246}, 2024.\n447\t\n448\t\\bibitem[Kornblith et~al.(2019)]{kornblith2019cka}\n449\tS.~Kornblith, M.~Norouzi, H.~Lee, and G.~Hinton.\n450\t\\newblock Similarity of Neural Network Representations Revisited.\n451\t\\newblock \\emph{ICML}, 2019.\n452\t\n453\t\\bibitem[Liu et~al.(2024)]{liu2024libero}\n454\tB.~Liu et~al.\n455\t\\newblock LIBERO: Benchmarking Knowledge Transfer for Lifelong Robot Learning.\n456\t\\newblock \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n457\t\n458\t\\bibitem[Lu et~al.(2025)]{lu2025probing}\n459\tY.~Lu et~al.\n460\t\\newblock Linear Probing Reveals Task Structure in VLA Hidden Layers.\n461\t\\newblock 2025.\n462\t\n463\t\\bibitem[Molinari et~al.(2025)]{molinari2025world}\n464\tM.~Molinari et~al.\n465\t\\newblock Emergent World Representations in Vision-Language-Action Models.\n466\t\\newblock 2025.\n467\t\n468\t\\bibitem[Oquab et~al.(2024)]{oquab2024dinov2}\n469\tM.~Oquab et~al.\n470\t\\newblock DINOv2: Learning Robust Visual Features without Supervision.\n471\t\\newblock \\emph{TMLR}, 2024.\n472\t\n473\t\\bibitem[Radford et~al.(2021)]{radford2021clip}\n474\tA.~Radford et~al.\n475\t\\newblock Learning Transferable Visual Models from Natural Language Supervision.\n476\t\\newblock \\emph{ICML}, 2021.\n477\t\n478\t\\bibitem[Rizzardo et~al.(2026)]{rizzardo2026observability}\n479\tC.~Rizzardo et~al.\n480\t\\newblock Feature Observability and Controllability in VLAs.\n481\t\\newblock 2026.\n482\t\n483\t\\bibitem[Swann et~al.(2026)]{swann2026sae}\n484\tE.~Swann et~al.\n485\t\\newblock Sparse Autoencoder Interpretability for OpenVLA.\n486\t\\newblock 2026.\n487\t\n488\t\\end{thebibliography}\n489\t\n490\t\\end{document}\n491\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WZxVFvGeKNdv939vUq8V6Z","session_id":"18723999-1838-4cd3-86c2-800e5e9f555a","uuid":"7ff4e2ef-64c2-4544-a7d2-3bef11062de9","timestamp":"2026-05-16T01:49:01.647Z"}
