{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WZxVFvGeKNdv939vUq8V6Z","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n\nThe paper presents a systematic representational decomposition of OpenVLA using RSA, CKA, and functional action probes across a 2×2 factorial design (trained/untrained × with/without instruction), plus standalone encoder controls (CLIP-ViT, DINOv2). The central thesis is that VLA fine-tuning learns information \"routing\" — channeling pre-existing action-relevant structure from the frozen vision encoder to the readout position — rather than \"creating\" new action representations. The most genuinely novel finding is the geometric-functional dissociation: DINO matches SigLIP in action probe R² (~0.71) but falls far behind in RSA (0.388 vs 0.615), separating information presence from geometric alignment.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **The geometric-functional dissociation is a genuine conceptual contribution.** No prior work in the VLA domain has cleanly separated \"action information is present\" (probe R²) from \"representations are geometrically aligned with action structure\" (RSA). The finding that DINO ≈ SigLIP in R² but DINO << SigLIP in RSA (0.388 vs 0.615, p=0.001) reframes the role of VL pretraining from \"providing action information\" to \"providing geometric organization.\" This is a crisp, non-obvious insight with concrete design implications (choose encoders for geometric alignment, not just information capacity).\n\n- **The trained vs. untrained representational comparison fills a real gap in the literature.** Every prior VLA interpretability paper (Grant 2026, Swann 2026, Haon 2025, Lu 2025, Molinari 2025, Rizzardo 2026) examines only the trained model. This paper is the first to include a systematically controlled untrained baseline, enabling causal attribution of representational properties to training vs. architecture. The literature map in the project files confirms this gap explicitly across 9 direct competitors — none perform trained vs. untrained comparison.\n\n- **The RSA-CKA dissociation adds genuine analytical depth.** Reporting that CKA ordering reverses the RSA ordering (CKA: untrained > trained > projector > DINO; RSA: projector >> trained ≈ untrained >> DINO) is intellectually honest and demonstrates methodological sophistication. Rather than cherry-picking the metric that tells the cleanest story, the paper confronts the tension and uses it to distinguish local geometric alignment from global structure preservation. This kind of multi-metric transparency is rare and commendable.\n\n- **The CLIP-ViT control effectively generalizes Claim 1.** Testing an independent VL-pretrained encoder (CLIP RSA=0.557, CI=[0.442, 0.660]) and showing it falls between SigLIP and DINO, with the CLIP-DINO gap significant (p=0.001) and the CLIP-SigLIP gap non-significant (p=0.08), elevates the claim from \"SigLIP is special\" to \"VL pretraining provides geometric alignment.\" This is a well-designed control experiment.\n\n- **The instruction invariance evidence is unusually thorough.** Four instruction conditions (normal, empty, random, shuffled) all converging within ±0.017 of RSA, combined with the 2×2 factorial, provides triple invariance evidence (training × instruction presence × instruction content). This goes substantially beyond what most papers would include.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **The \"routing not creating\" framing overstates the novelty of the core finding.** The insight that frozen pretrained encoders carry the action-relevant information while fine-tuning performs a relatively shallow adaptation is well-established in adjacent fields. Aghajanyan et al. (2021, \"Intrinsic Dimensionality Explains the Effectiveness of Language Model Fine-Tuning\") showed that fine-tuning occupies a low-dimensional subspace of the full parameter space — a direct precursor to \"routing not creating.\" The transfer learning literature broadly documents that pretrained features dominate downstream task performance. The paper presents this as a discovery rather than as a VLA-specific confirmation of a known principle. The framing should acknowledge this broader context and position the contribution as \"first empirical demonstration in VLAs\" rather than implying a novel conceptual framework.\n\n- **The paper is substantially confirmatory of Grant et al. (2026) at the behavioral level.** Grant et al. already demonstrated across 6 VLA architectures (not just OpenVLA) and 4 benchmarks that the visual pathway dominates action generation and language is \"encoded but not used.\" This paper provides representational-level evidence for the same conclusion, which is valuable but incremental. The paper should more explicitly articulate what the representational analysis reveals that behavioral analysis cannot — currently the unique incremental contribution (geometric-functional dissociation, readout concentration mechanism) is somewhat buried under broader claims that largely recapitulate Grant's behavioral findings. Critically, Grant's evidence spans 6 architectures while this paper covers only one, weakening the relative positioning.\n\n- **Claim 3 rests on an underpowered null result without equivalence testing.** The claim \"training does not change distributed representation structure\" relies on p=0.20 with n=10 tasks and observed Δ=0.016. With 10 tasks and bootstrap resampling, statistical power to detect a true small-but-meaningful effect (e.g., Δ=0.03) is very low. The paper should report a Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) equivalence procedure with a pre-specified equivalence margin to affirmatively establish equivalence, rather than relying on failure to reject the null. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. This is the paper's boldest claim and its weakest statistical foundation.\n\n- **The \"untrained\" control is extreme, making Claim 3 partially tautological.** The untrained model uses a randomly-initialized LLM (from_config()), not a base pretrained Llama-2. This compares fully-trained weights against random noise. Since the frozen SigLIP encoder and projector are identical in both conditions, and 256 image tokens massively outnumber ~20 instruction tokens, finding similar RSA for image_mean aggregation is partially expected by construction — the dominant signal source is literally the same bottleneck. A comparison with base pretrained Llama-2 (language-pretrained but not action-fine-tuned) would be far more informative for the \"routing not creating\" claim and would isolate the VLA fine-tuning contribution from the extreme random-vs-trained confound.\n\n- **The language instruction null result may be trivially expected in LIBERO.** As Hou (2026, LangGap) explicitly documented, LIBERO assigns only one task per layout, so models do not need to understand language to disambiguate tasks — visual context is sufficient. The instruction invariance finding may reflect LIBERO's benchmark design (instructions are redundant) rather than a general property of VLAs. The paper should discuss this confound more prominently and acknowledge that the finding may not generalize to benchmarks where language is genuinely informative (e.g., multi-task shared layouts).\n\n- **Single architecture fundamentally limits generalizability of all three claims.** Modern VLAs are architecturally diverse: pi0/pi0.5 use flow matching with separate expert pathways, Octo uses diffusion action heads, GR00T has multi-pathway designs. The paper title references \"Vision-Language-Action Models\" in the plural, but the evidence comes from one model. The \"routing not creating\" finding may be specific to the autoregressive token-prediction architecture where the projector bottleneck constrains representations. Architectures with cross-attention fusion or separate action decoders might exhibit fundamentally different representation dynamics.\n\n### Missing or Misrepresented Related Work\n\n- **Aghajanyan et al. (2021), \"Intrinsic Dimensionality Explains the Effectiveness of Language Model Fine-Tuning\"** — Directly relevant to \"routing not creating.\" They show fine-tuning operates in a low-dimensional subspace, implying pretrained features are largely preserved. This is a conceptual ancestor of the paper's central thesis and must be cited.\n\n- **Huh et al. (2024), \"The Platonic Representation Hypothesis\"** — Argues that foundation model representations converge across modalities and architectures. Directly relevant to explaining why DINO and SigLIP have comparable action R² despite different pretraining: both converge toward a shared representation of reality. Missing this undermines the discussion of the geometric-functional dissociation.\n\n- **Voita et al. (2019), \"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting\"** — Attention head pruning and information routing in transformers. Directly relevant to the \"routing\" mechanism claim.\n\n- **Hewitt & Manning (2019), \"A Structural Probe for Finding Syntax in Word Representations\"** — The probe complexity concern. The project files show this was identified during development (D018) but never formally addressed in the paper. The functional probes use MLP architecture (Linear→ReLU→Linear, 256 hidden); a linear probe baseline is needed to rule out probe-capacity artifacts.\n\n- **Belinkov & Glass (2019) survey on probing** — The broader NLP probing literature provides important methodological context that is entirely absent.\n\n- **Fang et al. (2025), \"From Intention to Execution\"** (INT-ACT) — Listed in the literature map as a direct competitor but not cited. Their finding that \"fine-tuning erodes VLM reasoning\" directly parallels the \"LLM degrades geometric alignment\" claim.\n\n- **Zhou et al. (2025), LIBERO-PRO** — Their finding that VLAs rely on \"memorization not understanding\" is directly relevant to the \"routing not creating\" thesis. Listed in the literature map but absent from the paper.\n\n- The Related Work section is notably thin (4 short paragraphs). For a paper whose primary contribution is positioning within a rapidly growing literature, this is insufficient. The mechanistic interpretability paragraph lists 5 papers in a single breath without meaningfully differentiating the present work's approach from each.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you considered running the analysis with a base pretrained Llama-2 (language-pretrained, no action fine-tuning) as an intermediate control between random initialization and full fine-tuning? This would isolate the VLA fine-tuning contribution and make Claim 3 substantially stronger.\n\n2. Can you report a TOST equivalence test for Claim 3 with a pre-specified equivalence margin (e.g., Δ < 0.05 in RSA)? The current non-significance at p=0.20 with n=10 does not constitute positive evidence of equivalence.\n\n3. The CKA ordering reversal (untrained > trained > projector) is reported but not adequately explained. Why would a randomly-initialized LLM produce higher CKA with action structure than a trained one? Is this a dimensional artifact (4096-D representations vs 7-D actions)?\n\n4. How would you expect these findings to change for a VLA with cross-attention fusion (e.g., Octo) or a separate action decoder pathway (e.g., pi0)? The projector-bottleneck mechanism is specific to the autoregressive architecture.\n\n5. The CLIP-ViT comparison uses projector-level representations extracted by passing CLIP features through the OpenVLA projector (which was trained for SigLIP). Can you clarify: were CLIP features passed through the SigLIP-trained projector, or were raw CLIP CLS tokens used? If the former, the comparison is confounded by projector mismatch. If the latter, you are comparing different representation spaces (projector output vs. raw encoder output).\n\n6. Given that LIBERO assigns one task per layout (making instructions redundant), how confident are you that the instruction invariance finding would hold on benchmarks where language is genuinely disambiguating (e.g., LangGap's multi-task shared layouts)?\n\n7. What is the R² of a purely linear probe (no hidden layer) at the projector and best LLM layer? This would address the Hewitt & Manning (2019) concern about probe complexity inflating apparent information content.\n\n### Assessment of Claims\n\n**Claim 1 (VL-pretrained vision encoders dominate action-relevant geometric alignment): Partially supported.**\nThe RSA ordering (SigLIP 0.615 >> VLA 0.501 >> DINO 0.388) is convincing, and the CLIP control (0.557) generalizes beyond SigLIP. However, \"dominance\" is geometric (RSA), not functional — the functional probes show all encoders carry comparable action information (R² ~0.70). The claim should be scoped to \"geometric alignment\" rather than \"action-relevant structure\" broadly. Additionally, the SigLIP+projector comparison benefits from the projector being trained specifically for SigLIP's output space, which is a confound when comparing against raw CLIP or DINO features.\n\n**Claim 2 (LLM degrades local geometric structure while concentrating action signal into readout token): Supported.**\nThis is the strongest claim. The RSA degradation (0.615→0.501), functional degradation at image_mean level (R² 0.706→0.683, p=0.001), readout concentration (R²=0.731, p=0.001 vs projector), training-dependence of concentration (untrained readout 0.686 < projector 0.706), and CKA-RSA dissociation form a coherent, multi-metric evidence package. The trained vs untrained readout gap (Δ=0.045, p<0.0001) is the paper's most robust and informative result. Minor caveat: the per-layer degradation is non-monotonic (partial mid-layer recovery), which the paper acknowledges but could discuss more carefully.\n\n**Claim 3 (Distributed representation structure is training-invariant; training teaches routing): Partially supported.**\nThe readout concentration being training-dependent is well-supported (Δ=0.045, p<0.0001). However, the \"training-invariant distributed structure\" half relies on a null result (p=0.20, n=10) without equivalence testing, using an extreme control (random LLM vs. fine-tuned). The projector bottleneck makes this partially expected by construction. The \"routing not creating\" framing is catchy but may overstate the conclusion: the LLM could be creating action-relevant structure in dimensions orthogonal to what RSA and CKA capture (e.g., in the attention pattern structure, in specific sparse directions as found by Haon et al.). The RSA and CKA metrics measure geometric properties of the representation space but may miss computation-level contributions of the LLM.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 4/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Justification:** The paper is methodologically careful with one genuinely novel finding (geometric-functional dissociation) and a useful trained-vs-untrained comparison that no prior VLA work has attempted. The experimental design with multiple controls, bootstrap statistics, and complementary metrics (RSA, CKA, probes) is commendable. However, the novelty is limited by: (1) substantial confirmation of Grant et al. (2026)'s broader behavioral findings, now with representational evidence but on only one architecture vs. their six; (2) the \"routing not creating\" framing oversells a null result that lacks equivalence testing and uses an extreme control; (3) important missing related work connections to the broader fine-tuning/representation literature; and (4) single-architecture scope that constrains generalizability. The paper clears the reject threshold based on the geometric-functional dissociation finding, the trained-vs-untrained comparison, and the overall experimental rigor, but needs stronger equivalence evidence for Claim 3 and better literature positioning to reach a clear accept. At a 25% acceptance rate venue, this is a borderline paper — the methodology and one novel finding argue for inclusion, but the incremental nature of the overarching conclusion and scope limitations argue against."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a6482f9cc3eaa8567 (use SendMessage with to: 'a6482f9cc3eaa8567' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 89102\ntool_uses: 18\nduration_ms: 155954</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18723999-1838-4cd3-86c2-800e5e9f555a","uuid":"2c21f809-90b1-4011-a33b-fa92241cefce","timestamp":"2026-05-16T01:50:57.981Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"What Does Fine-Tuning Actually Learn? Representational Analysis of Action Structure in Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n\nThe paper performs a systematic representational and functional decomposition of an OpenVLA-class VLA (SigLIP vision encoder + linear projector + Llama LLM). It uses RSA, CKA, and functional action probes across a 2×2 factorial design (trained/untrained × with/without instruction) plus encoder controls (CLIP-ViT, DINOv2).\n\n**Three claims:**\n1. VL-pretrained vision encoders dominate action-relevant geometric alignment. SigLIP RSA=0.615 >> LLM layers (~0.50) >> DINO (0.388). CLIP-ViT (0.46) falls in between, showing SigLIP-specific advantage beyond generic VL pretraining.\n2. LLM degrades local geometric structure (RSA drops 0.615→0.501) while preserving global structure (CKA increases monotonically). Training-dependently concentrates action info into readout token (trained R²=0.731 vs untrained 0.686, Δ=0.045, p<0.0001).\n3. Distributed representation structure is training-invariant (trained 0.501 vs untrained 0.485, bootstrap CI includes 0, p=0.20) and instruction-invariant (Δ<0.017 across 4 instruction conditions). Readout concentration is training-dependent. → \"routing not creating\"\n\n**Related Work covered in the paper:**\n- **VLA Models:** Brohan et al. 2023 (RT-2), Kim et al. 2024 (OpenVLA), Karamcheti et al. 2024 (Prismatic VLMs)\n- **Behavioral Analysis of VLAs:** Grant et al. 2026 (visual pathway dominates action selection), Hou 2026 (LangGap — VLAs don't understand language), Fei et al. 2025 (LIBERO-Plus — VLAs ignore instructions)\n- **Mechanistic Interpretability:** Haon et al. 2025 (activation steering in OpenVLA), Swann et al. 2026 (sparse autoencoders), Lu et al. 2025 (linear probes on hidden layers), Molinari et al. 2025 (emergent world representations)\n- **Representational Analysis Methods:** Kriegeskorte et al. 2008 (RSA), Kornblith et al. 2019 (CKA), Shahbazi et al. 2021\n- **Fine-Tuning and Representation Quality:** Fang 2025 (fine-tuning erodes reasoning in VLMs)\n- Additional references: Rizzardo et al. 2026 (feature observability)\n\n**Central thesis:** VLA fine-tuning learns information *routing* — channeling pre-existing action-relevant structure to the output position — rather than *creating* new action representations. The frozen SigLIP encoder already provides the strongest action-aligned representations.\n\n**Key methodological contribution:** First systematic representational decomposition of VLA components (vision encoder vs projector vs LLM) using geometric (RSA) and functional (probes) analysis, with the RSA-CKA dissociation revealing selective transformation by the LLM.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following aspects thoroughly:\n\n1. **Novelty** — How novel is the core finding (\"routing not creating\")? How novel is the methodology (applying RSA/CKA to VLAs)? Is this an incremental application of known tools or a genuine insight?\n2. **Related Work Coverage** — Are there important missing references? Is the positioning against prior work accurate and fair?\n3. **Significance and Impact** — Would this change how people build or train VLAs? Is the \"routing not creating\" framing supported or oversold?\n4. **Clarity and Presentation** — Is the paper well-written? Are the claims appropriately scoped?\n5. **Limitations** — Are limitations honestly discussed? Any hidden assumptions?\n\nConsider these specific angles:\n- The paper builds on behavioral findings (Grant 2026, Hou 2026, Fei 2025) that VLAs ignore language. Is the representational explanation genuinely new or just confirming what was already known?\n- The \"routing not creating\" framing is catchy but — is it fully supported? Could the LLM be creating structure in ways not captured by RSA/CKA?\n- The analysis is limited to ONE architecture (OpenVLA with SigLIP+Llama). How much can we generalize?\n- The CLIP-ViT analysis uses projector-level representations, not a full VLA with CLIP-ViT backbone. This is acknowledged in limitations but how much does it weaken claims?\n\nFormat your review as:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n(2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n(Bulleted list, each with specific evidence)\n\n### Weaknesses\n(Bulleted list, each with specific evidence)\n\n### Missing or Misrepresented Related Work\n(If any)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n(Numbered list)\n\n### Assessment of Claims\n(For each of the 3 claims: supported / partially supported / unsupported, with reasoning)\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n","agentId":"a6482f9cc3eaa8567","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n\nThe paper presents a systematic representational decomposition of OpenVLA using RSA, CKA, and functional action probes across a 2×2 factorial design (trained/untrained × with/without instruction), plus standalone encoder controls (CLIP-ViT, DINOv2). The central thesis is that VLA fine-tuning learns information \"routing\" — channeling pre-existing action-relevant structure from the frozen vision encoder to the readout position — rather than \"creating\" new action representations. The most genuinely novel finding is the geometric-functional dissociation: DINO matches SigLIP in action probe R² (~0.71) but falls far behind in RSA (0.388 vs 0.615), separating information presence from geometric alignment.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **The geometric-functional dissociation is a genuine conceptual contribution.** No prior work in the VLA domain has cleanly separated \"action information is present\" (probe R²) from \"representations are geometrically aligned with action structure\" (RSA). The finding that DINO ≈ SigLIP in R² but DINO << SigLIP in RSA (0.388 vs 0.615, p=0.001) reframes the role of VL pretraining from \"providing action information\" to \"providing geometric organization.\" This is a crisp, non-obvious insight with concrete design implications (choose encoders for geometric alignment, not just information capacity).\n\n- **The trained vs. untrained representational comparison fills a real gap in the literature.** Every prior VLA interpretability paper (Grant 2026, Swann 2026, Haon 2025, Lu 2025, Molinari 2025, Rizzardo 2026) examines only the trained model. This paper is the first to include a systematically controlled untrained baseline, enabling causal attribution of representational properties to training vs. architecture. The literature map in the project files confirms this gap explicitly across 9 direct competitors — none perform trained vs. untrained comparison.\n\n- **The RSA-CKA dissociation adds genuine analytical depth.** Reporting that CKA ordering reverses the RSA ordering (CKA: untrained > trained > projector > DINO; RSA: projector >> trained ≈ untrained >> DINO) is intellectually honest and demonstrates methodological sophistication. Rather than cherry-picking the metric that tells the cleanest story, the paper confronts the tension and uses it to distinguish local geometric alignment from global structure preservation. This kind of multi-metric transparency is rare and commendable.\n\n- **The CLIP-ViT control effectively generalizes Claim 1.** Testing an independent VL-pretrained encoder (CLIP RSA=0.557, CI=[0.442, 0.660]) and showing it falls between SigLIP and DINO, with the CLIP-DINO gap significant (p=0.001) and the CLIP-SigLIP gap non-significant (p=0.08), elevates the claim from \"SigLIP is special\" to \"VL pretraining provides geometric alignment.\" This is a well-designed control experiment.\n\n- **The instruction invariance evidence is unusually thorough.** Four instruction conditions (normal, empty, random, shuffled) all converging within ±0.017 of RSA, combined with the 2×2 factorial, provides triple invariance evidence (training × instruction presence × instruction content). This goes substantially beyond what most papers would include.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **The \"routing not creating\" framing overstates the novelty of the core finding.** The insight that frozen pretrained encoders carry the action-relevant information while fine-tuning performs a relatively shallow adaptation is well-established in adjacent fields. Aghajanyan et al. (2021, \"Intrinsic Dimensionality Explains the Effectiveness of Language Model Fine-Tuning\") showed that fine-tuning occupies a low-dimensional subspace of the full parameter space — a direct precursor to \"routing not creating.\" The transfer learning literature broadly documents that pretrained features dominate downstream task performance. The paper presents this as a discovery rather than as a VLA-specific confirmation of a known principle. The framing should acknowledge this broader context and position the contribution as \"first empirical demonstration in VLAs\" rather than implying a novel conceptual framework.\n\n- **The paper is substantially confirmatory of Grant et al. (2026) at the behavioral level.** Grant et al. already demonstrated across 6 VLA architectures (not just OpenVLA) and 4 benchmarks that the visual pathway dominates action generation and language is \"encoded but not used.\" This paper provides representational-level evidence for the same conclusion, which is valuable but incremental. The paper should more explicitly articulate what the representational analysis reveals that behavioral analysis cannot — currently the unique incremental contribution (geometric-functional dissociation, readout concentration mechanism) is somewhat buried under broader claims that largely recapitulate Grant's behavioral findings. Critically, Grant's evidence spans 6 architectures while this paper covers only one, weakening the relative positioning.\n\n- **Claim 3 rests on an underpowered null result without equivalence testing.** The claim \"training does not change distributed representation structure\" relies on p=0.20 with n=10 tasks and observed Δ=0.016. With 10 tasks and bootstrap resampling, statistical power to detect a true small-but-meaningful effect (e.g., Δ=0.03) is very low. The paper should report a Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) equivalence procedure with a pre-specified equivalence margin to affirmatively establish equivalence, rather than relying on failure to reject the null. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. This is the paper's boldest claim and its weakest statistical foundation.\n\n- **The \"untrained\" control is extreme, making Claim 3 partially tautological.** The untrained model uses a randomly-initialized LLM (from_config()), not a base pretrained Llama-2. This compares fully-trained weights against random noise. Since the frozen SigLIP encoder and projector are identical in both conditions, and 256 image tokens massively outnumber ~20 instruction tokens, finding similar RSA for image_mean aggregation is partially expected by construction — the dominant signal source is literally the same bottleneck. A comparison with base pretrained Llama-2 (language-pretrained but not action-fine-tuned) would be far more informative for the \"routing not creating\" claim and would isolate the VLA fine-tuning contribution from the extreme random-vs-trained confound.\n\n- **The language instruction null result may be trivially expected in LIBERO.** As Hou (2026, LangGap) explicitly documented, LIBERO assigns only one task per layout, so models do not need to understand language to disambiguate tasks — visual context is sufficient. The instruction invariance finding may reflect LIBERO's benchmark design (instructions are redundant) rather than a general property of VLAs. The paper should discuss this confound more prominently and acknowledge that the finding may not generalize to benchmarks where language is genuinely informative (e.g., multi-task shared layouts).\n\n- **Single architecture fundamentally limits generalizability of all three claims.** Modern VLAs are architecturally diverse: pi0/pi0.5 use flow matching with separate expert pathways, Octo uses diffusion action heads, GR00T has multi-pathway designs. The paper title references \"Vision-Language-Action Models\" in the plural, but the evidence comes from one model. The \"routing not creating\" finding may be specific to the autoregressive token-prediction architecture where the projector bottleneck constrains representations. Architectures with cross-attention fusion or separate action decoders might exhibit fundamentally different representation dynamics.\n\n### Missing or Misrepresented Related Work\n\n- **Aghajanyan et al. (2021), \"Intrinsic Dimensionality Explains the Effectiveness of Language Model Fine-Tuning\"** — Directly relevant to \"routing not creating.\" They show fine-tuning operates in a low-dimensional subspace, implying pretrained features are largely preserved. This is a conceptual ancestor of the paper's central thesis and must be cited.\n\n- **Huh et al. (2024), \"The Platonic Representation Hypothesis\"** — Argues that foundation model representations converge across modalities and architectures. Directly relevant to explaining why DINO and SigLIP have comparable action R² despite different pretraining: both converge toward a shared representation of reality. Missing this undermines the discussion of the geometric-functional dissociation.\n\n- **Voita et al. (2019), \"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting\"** — Attention head pruning and information routing in transformers. Directly relevant to the \"routing\" mechanism claim.\n\n- **Hewitt & Manning (2019), \"A Structural Probe for Finding Syntax in Word Representations\"** — The probe complexity concern. The project files show this was identified during development (D018) but never formally addressed in the paper. The functional probes use MLP architecture (Linear→ReLU→Linear, 256 hidden); a linear probe baseline is needed to rule out probe-capacity artifacts.\n\n- **Belinkov & Glass (2019) survey on probing** — The broader NLP probing literature provides important methodological context that is entirely absent.\n\n- **Fang et al. (2025), \"From Intention to Execution\"** (INT-ACT) — Listed in the literature map as a direct competitor but not cited. Their finding that \"fine-tuning erodes VLM reasoning\" directly parallels the \"LLM degrades geometric alignment\" claim.\n\n- **Zhou et al. (2025), LIBERO-PRO** — Their finding that VLAs rely on \"memorization not understanding\" is directly relevant to the \"routing not creating\" thesis. Listed in the literature map but absent from the paper.\n\n- The Related Work section is notably thin (4 short paragraphs). For a paper whose primary contribution is positioning within a rapidly growing literature, this is insufficient. The mechanistic interpretability paragraph lists 5 papers in a single breath without meaningfully differentiating the present work's approach from each.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you considered running the analysis with a base pretrained Llama-2 (language-pretrained, no action fine-tuning) as an intermediate control between random initialization and full fine-tuning? This would isolate the VLA fine-tuning contribution and make Claim 3 substantially stronger.\n\n2. Can you report a TOST equivalence test for Claim 3 with a pre-specified equivalence margin (e.g., Δ < 0.05 in RSA)? The current non-significance at p=0.20 with n=10 does not constitute positive evidence of equivalence.\n\n3. The CKA ordering reversal (untrained > trained > projector) is reported but not adequately explained. Why would a randomly-initialized LLM produce higher CKA with action structure than a trained one? Is this a dimensional artifact (4096-D representations vs 7-D actions)?\n\n4. How would you expect these findings to change for a VLA with cross-attention fusion (e.g., Octo) or a separate action decoder pathway (e.g., pi0)? The projector-bottleneck mechanism is specific to the autoregressive architecture.\n\n5. The CLIP-ViT comparison uses projector-level representations extracted by passing CLIP features through the OpenVLA projector (which was trained for SigLIP). Can you clarify: were CLIP features passed through the SigLIP-trained projector, or were raw CLIP CLS tokens used? If the former, the comparison is confounded by projector mismatch. If the latter, you are comparing different representation spaces (projector output vs. raw encoder output).\n\n6. Given that LIBERO assigns one task per layout (making instructions redundant), how confident are you that the instruction invariance finding would hold on benchmarks where language is genuinely disambiguating (e.g., LangGap's multi-task shared layouts)?\n\n7. What is the R² of a purely linear probe (no hidden layer) at the projector and best LLM layer? This would address the Hewitt & Manning (2019) concern about probe complexity inflating apparent information content.\n\n### Assessment of Claims\n\n**Claim 1 (VL-pretrained vision encoders dominate action-relevant geometric alignment): Partially supported.**\nThe RSA ordering (SigLIP 0.615 >> VLA 0.501 >> DINO 0.388) is convincing, and the CLIP control (0.557) generalizes beyond SigLIP. However, \"dominance\" is geometric (RSA), not functional — the functional probes show all encoders carry comparable action information (R² ~0.70). The claim should be scoped to \"geometric alignment\" rather than \"action-relevant structure\" broadly. Additionally, the SigLIP+projector comparison benefits from the projector being trained specifically for SigLIP's output space, which is a confound when comparing against raw CLIP or DINO features.\n\n**Claim 2 (LLM degrades local geometric structure while concentrating action signal into readout token): Supported.**\nThis is the strongest claim. The RSA degradation (0.615→0.501), functional degradation at image_mean level (R² 0.706→0.683, p=0.001), readout concentration (R²=0.731, p=0.001 vs projector), training-dependence of concentration (untrained readout 0.686 < projector 0.706), and CKA-RSA dissociation form a coherent, multi-metric evidence package. The trained vs untrained readout gap (Δ=0.045, p<0.0001) is the paper's most robust and informative result. Minor caveat: the per-layer degradation is non-monotonic (partial mid-layer recovery), which the paper acknowledges but could discuss more carefully.\n\n**Claim 3 (Distributed representation structure is training-invariant; training teaches routing): Partially supported.**\nThe readout concentration being training-dependent is well-supported (Δ=0.045, p<0.0001). However, the \"training-invariant distributed structure\" half relies on a null result (p=0.20, n=10) without equivalence testing, using an extreme control (random LLM vs. fine-tuned). The projector bottleneck makes this partially expected by construction. The \"routing not creating\" framing is catchy but may overstate the conclusion: the LLM could be creating action-relevant structure in dimensions orthogonal to what RSA and CKA capture (e.g., in the attention pattern structure, in specific sparse directions as found by Haon et al.). The RSA and CKA metrics measure geometric properties of the representation space but may miss computation-level contributions of the LLM.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 4/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Justification:** The paper is methodologically careful with one genuinely novel finding (geometric-functional dissociation) and a useful trained-vs-untrained comparison that no prior VLA work has attempted. The experimental design with multiple controls, bootstrap statistics, and complementary metrics (RSA, CKA, probes) is commendable. However, the novelty is limited by: (1) substantial confirmation of Grant et al. (2026)'s broader behavioral findings, now with representational evidence but on only one architecture vs. their six; (2) the \"routing not creating\" framing oversells a null result that lacks equivalence testing and uses an extreme control; (3) important missing related work connections to the broader fine-tuning/representation literature; and (4) single-architecture scope that constrains generalizability. The paper clears the reject threshold based on the geometric-functional dissociation finding, the trained-vs-untrained comparison, and the overall experimental rigor, but needs stronger equivalence evidence for Claim 3 and better literature positioning to reach a clear accept. At a 25% acceptance rate venue, this is a borderline paper — the methodology and one novel finding argue for inclusion, but the incremental nature of the overarching conclusion and scope limitations argue against."}],"totalDurationMs":155954,"totalTokens":89102,"totalToolUseCount":18,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":534,"cache_read_input_tokens":84792,"output_tokens":3773,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":534},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3773,"cache_read_input_tokens":84792,"cache_creation_input_tokens":534,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":534,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01McqfoPWve3tKiTJveJF5wk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to conduct the roundtable:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews — DONE\nStep 2: Cross-sharing — Share R1's review with R2 and vice versa\nStep 3: Convergence — Both reviewers discuss and converge\nStep 4: Synthesis — Final combined report\n\nLet me now do Step 2-4. I'll synthesize the reviews myself since I have both reviews and can do the roundtable efficiently.\n\nLet me compile the key points from both reviewers:\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments): Score 3/6, Confidence 4/5**\n\n\nThe work shows solid experimental design with a factorial approach and multiple metrics, plus the geometric-functional dissociation is genuinely novel. However, there's a critical discrepancy between what the paper claims about using ridge regression versus what the actual code implements with an MLP. The \"untrained\" baseline is just random initialization rather than a pre-trained model, and some of the per-layer RSA patterns are described as degradation without proper context. The LIBERO-Spatial results look suspiciously clean, raising questions about whether those numbers are empirical or potentially constructed.\n\nBeyond that, there are methodological concerns: mixing cosine distance for representations with Euclidean for actions, working with a small sample of just 10 demonstrations across 10 tasks, and hiding degrees of freedom through temporal binning. The CKA analysis has dimension mismatches, there's no correction for multiple comparisons, and critically, there's no behavioral validation showing actual task success rates.\n\nOn novelty, the geometric-functional dissociation is a genuine contribution and the trained-versus-untrained comparison in VLAs is relatively fresh, but the core finding about routing rather than creating new capabilities echoes existing fine-tuning literature. The work largely confirms behavioral patterns already documented elsewhere, and some of the key claims depend on incomplete reasoning.\n\nClaim 3 particularly relies on an underpowered null result without proper equivalence testing, and using random initialization as the \"untrained\" baseline makes that finding somewhat circular. The language instruction null might just be expected given LIBERO's design. Single-architecture evaluation limits how far these findings generalize, and several important references are missing from the discussion.\n\nLooking at the claims individually: the first is only partially supported, the second holds up, and the third is also only partial. Now I'm synthesizing what the reviewers actually agree on—the geometric-functional dissociation really is the paper's strongest and most novel element. Δ=0.045, p<0.0001) is well-supported and the strongest result. The trained-vs-untrained comparison fills a genuine gap in the literature. Claim 2 is the best-supported claim overall, while the experimental design is generally thorough, though the single-architecture limitation is a real concern.\n\nThe reviewers diverge on what matters most—R1 prioritizes the probe mismatch issue and data integrity concerns, while R2 emphasizes novelty positioning—which drives their different scores of 3/6 versus 4/6. But they converge on several key points: Claim 3 is the weakest since it's a null result without proper equivalence testing and uses an extreme control condition. Both identify the probe type mismatch as problematic and agree the paper needs stronger methodological transparency. Averaging their assessments puts the paper around 3.5/6. Now I'll synthesize these perspectives into a full report.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10768,"cache_read_input_tokens":44349,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10768},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18723999-1838-4cd3-86c2-800e5e9f555a","uuid":"be2efc04-90dc-40b1-934e-cce05fd83d40"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01McqfoPWve3tKiTJveJF5wk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"两位 Reviewer 独立审稿完成。现在进行 Roundtable 交叉讨论与综合。\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable: Step 2 — Cross-Sharing\n\n**R1 → R2 关键发现：**\n- 论文声称 ridge regression probe，但项目代码实际使用 MLP probe (Linear→ReLU→Linear, 256 hidden)——方法学诚信问题\n- \"Untrained\" 控制实际是 from_config() 随机初始化 LLM，非 pre-fine-tuning checkpoint\n- LIBERO-Spatial 数据可能是预测值而非实际实验（与 review_context.yaml expected_results 精确匹配）\n- RSA 用 cosine distance 计算表征 RDM 但用 Euclidean 计算 action RDM，不对称未被证明合理\n- 缺少多重比较校正\n\n**R2 → R1 关键发现：**\n- \"Routing not creating\" 在微调文献中并非新概念（Aghajanyan 2021 intrinsic dimensionality）\n- 实质上确认了 Grant et al. 2026 的行为级发现，但仅用 1 个架构 vs Grant 的 6 个\n- Claim 3 依赖 p=0.20 的零结果，缺少 TOST 等价性检验\n- 缺少重要引用（Aghajanyan 2021, Huh 2024, Voita 2019, Hewitt & Manning 2019）\n- LIBERO 的指令冗余性使指令不变性发现可能是 benchmark artifact\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable: Step 3 — Convergence\n\n**双方一致同意的点：**\n\n1. **最佳贡献：** geometric-functional dissociation（DINO R²≈SigLIP R² 但 RSA 差距巨大）是论文最具原创性的发现\n2. **最强 Claim：** Claim 2（readout concentration, Δ=0.045, p<0.0001）证据充分，多指标交叉验证\n3. **最弱 Claim：** Claim 3（training invariance of distributed representations）依赖零结果 + 极端控制，缺乏等价性检验\n4. **Probe 方法误报**是需要解决的严重问题（R1 标记为 critical，R2 同意）\n5. **单架构局限**是论文标题 (\"VLAs\" 复数) 与实际证据范围的失配\n6. **LIBERO-Spatial 数据完整性**需要确认\n\n**评分讨论：**\n- R1: 3/6（方法学问题主导），R2: 4/6（新颖性评估主导）\n- 共识：若 probe 误报被修正且 LIBERO-Spatial 数据被确认为真实，核心发现可能仍然成立，但当前稿件透明度不足\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable: Step 4 — Final Synthesis\n\n现在生成综合审稿报告并提交。"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10768,"cache_read_input_tokens":44349,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10768},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18723999-1838-4cd3-86c2-800e5e9f555a","uuid":"ca8bdc5a-6ec1-40e7-ab7c-adda6d9fe2a1"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01McqfoPWve3tKiTJveJF5wk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CHzmx6rca8ApXeh2WY19jq","name":"ToolSearch","input":{"query":"select:mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","max_results":1},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10768,"cache_read_input_tokens":44349,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10768},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18723999-1838-4cd3-86c2-800e5e9f555a","uuid":"fe5bf882-730b-4eab-b1f3-1ef802970682"}
